sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch06-21> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "As cellular automata evolve, the set of cell sequences that can occur generally becomes progressively restricted; the finite networks describing these sets stay simple (about t^2 nodes after t steps) for class 1 and 2 rules but grow at least exponentially for most class 3 and 4 rules, apart from surjective rules started from all possible sequences.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Allowed-sequence networks grow fast for class 3 and 4, slowly for 1 and 2";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 6, “Starting from Randomness”; section “The Notion of Attractors” (from p. 275), p. 279. Scope: one-dimensional cellular automata and the regular languages of their configurations"
}
